WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.146
Cloud incident response combines traditional incident response discipline with cloud-native concerns.

2
00:00:07.146 --> 00:00:25.287
The familiar phases of detection, analysis, containment, recovery, and lessons learned still apply, but the evidence and control points often involve identities, APIs, audit logs, policies, storage, managed services, workloads, automation, and provider boundaries.

3
00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:28.036
Strong response depends on preparation.

4
00:00:28.036 --> 00:00:40.366
Teams need asset inventory, identity inventory, logging, centralized evidence, retention, runbooks, scoped access, response roles, and escalation paths before a serious incident.

5
00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:46.413
Without visibility, responders may be forced to make decisions from incomplete evidence.

6
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:51.046
With preparation, they can scope faster and act with more confidence.

7
00:00:51.046 --> 00:00:56.622
Identity often deserves early attention because cloud access is identity-driven.

8
00:00:56.622 --> 00:01:02.512
Compromised users, service accounts, tokens, and access keys can affect many services.

9
00:01:02.512 --> 00:01:14.527
Storage, workloads, and network configuration also require careful analysis because containment actions can preserve evidence or destroy it depending on how they are performed.

10
00:01:14.527 --> 00:01:17.904
The goal is not only to stop the immediate incident.

11
00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:26.543
The goal is to preserve evidence, understand impact, restore trusted service, communicate responsibly, and reduce recurrence.

12
00:01:26.543 --> 00:01:36.124
Cloud response must balance urgency with documentation, legal and privacy coordination, business continuity, and safe recovery sequencing.

13
00:01:36.124 --> 00:01:42.171
The practical takeaway is to make cloud response observable, authorized, and repeatable.

14
00:01:42.171 --> 00:01:56.856
Know your scope, know your logs, know your identities, preserve what matters, contain with care, recover from trusted sources, and convert lessons learned into better architecture, detections, runbooks, and training.
